title: Choosing DSL syntax
tags: Query
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: Free punctuation is scarce and reserved; new syntax first looks for a free grammatical position, then a named operator, and a sigil last.

The punctuation still free in the DSL ''and'' the simplified language
([[Simplified query language]]) is a scarce,
deliberately reserved resource. `#` (and `#=`, `##=`) belong to tags in the
simplified language, `~` to its `MATCHES`, `+` to its `OR` — and `&` is kept free on
purpose, as `+`'s natural companion for a future explicit `AND` there. The other
candidates already mean something: `$` is the grammar file's metasyntax, `:` separates
a field from its aspect (and a field spec's type), `*` belongs to the arrows,
`|` `^` `%` read as or, anchor and wildcard.

So new syntax goes, in order of preference:

# to a ''free position in the grammar'' — the bare uuid atom costs no sigil at all: a predicate always began with a field name, so a lone 32-hex token where a predicate is expected was free;
# to a ''named operator'', recognised only when immediately followed by `(` so that a field of that name stays usable — `osm(…)`, `same(…)`, and `uuid_in(…)` rather than the `uuid_in [...]` once proposed, which would have spent `[` and `]`;
# to a sigil only last — and then preferably in the ''simplified'' grammar, which the user can edit, so the choice is one line of configuration away from being undone, rather than in the core DSL.

The inverse arrow `<-` is an example of a spelling lost to an earlier one: `size<-5`
already means `size < -5` ([[Same-value matching]]).
